    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L05-ACTIVITY-010",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: Card Sort: Two Types of Coordinates",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We've learned how to transform functions in several ways. We can translate graphs of functions up and down, changing the output values while keeping the input values. We can translate graphs left and right, changing the input values while…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L05-ACTIVITY-011",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: Card Sort: Two Types of Coordinates",
      "SourceExcerpt": "For some functions, we can perform specific transformations and it looks like we didn't do anything at all. Consider the function \\(f\\) whose graph is shown here:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L05-ACTIVITY-012",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: Card Sort: Two Types of Coordinates",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What transformation could we do to the graph of \\(f\\) that would result in the same graph? Examining the shape of the graph, we can see a symmetry between points to the left of the \\(y\\)-axis and the points to the right of the \\(y\\)-axis.…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L05-ACTIVITY-013",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: Card Sort: Two Types of Coordinates",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Now consider the function \\(g\\) whose graph is shown here:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L05-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: Card Sort: Two Types of Coordinates",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What transformation could we do to the graph of \\(g\\) that would result in the same graph? Examining the shape of the graph, we can see that there is a symmetry between points on opposite sides of the axes. Looking at the points on the…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L05-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: Card Sort: Two Types of Coordinates",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A function \\(f\\) that satisfies the condition \\(f(x) = f(\\text-x)\\) for all inputs \\(x\\). You can tell an even function from its graph: Its graph is symmetric about the \\(y\\)-axis.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L05-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: Card Sort: Two Types of Coordinates",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A function \\(f\\) that satisfies \\(f(x) = \\text-f(\\text-x)\\) for all inputs \\(x\\). You can tell an odd function from its graph: Its graph is taken to itself when you reflect it across both the \\(x\\)- and \\(y\\)-axes. This can also be seen as…",
